.TH LX 1 "August 2026" "LX 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lx \- inspect and manage related Linux resources
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lx
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ command-options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
LX provides a unified view of processes, listening sockets, systemd services,
and journal entries. It uses procfs, Netlink, pidfd, sd-bus, and sd-journal
directly and does not invoke traditional administration commands.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-json
Write stable schema version 1 JSON for read-only commands.
.TP
.BR \-\-quiet
Suppress non-essential diagnostics and successful action messages.
.TP
.BR \-\-no-color
Disable ANSI color output. A non-empty
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable has the same effect.
.TP
.BR \-\-version
Print the LX version.
.TP
.BR \-h , " \-\-help"
Show help.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Show sampled host CPU usage, memory usage, and uptime.
.TP
.BR "process" " [PID] [--name NAME] [--user USER|UID] [--service UNIT]"
List processes or inspect one PID. Process CPU follows top-style semantics and
may exceed 100 percent on multi-core systems. Use
.B --raw-command
with a PID to disable best-effort command secret redaction.
.TP
.BR "process stop" " PID"
Send SIGTERM after validating the target.
.TP
.BR "process kill" " PID [--yes]"
Send SIGKILL. Confirmation is required unless
.B --yes
is supplied. PID 1 and the running LX process are always rejected.
.TP
.BR "port" " [PORT]"
List listening TCP sockets and unconnected UDP bindings, or inspect a local
port. Visible process and service owners are included.
.TP
.BR "port free" " PORT [--yes]"
Release a port using a revalidated ownership snapshot. LX prefers stopping one
common systemd service; otherwise it sends SIGTERM and optionally SIGKILL.
.TP
.BR "service" " [UNIT] [start|stop|restart] [--yes]"
List, inspect, or control systemd services. The
.B svc
alias is also accepted. Missing unit suffixes are normalized to
.B .service
and stop or restart requires confirmation unless
.B --yes
is supplied.
.TP
.BR "log" " [UNIT] [--pid PID] [--lines N] [--since TIME] [--follow]"
Read journal entries filtered by service, PID, or both. Relative times use
.BR 30s , " 10m" , " 2h" ,
or
.BR 3d ;
absolute local timestamps use
.BR "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS" .
Follow mode exits with status 130 after Ctrl-C.
.TP
.BR "inspect" " RESOURCE"
Inspect a related resource graph. Explicit forms are
.BR port:PORT , " pid:PID" ,
and
.BR service:UNIT .
.TP
.BR "find" " QUERY"
Search currently observable services, processes, ports, and executable paths.
.TP
.B doctor
Report runtime capability and backend availability.
.TP
.B tui
Open the optional interactive dashboard. TUI-enabled builds also open it when
run without arguments from an interactive terminal.
.SH TUI KEYS
.TP
.BR Tab , " Left" , " Right"
Switch between Services, Ports, and Processes.
.TP
.BR Up , " Down"
Select a resource.
.TP
.B Enter
Inspect the selected resource.
.TP
.BR / , " l"
Find resources or show recent logs.
.TP
.BR s , " r"
Confirm stopping or restarting the selected service.
.TP
.BR k , " K"
Confirm SIGTERM or double-confirm SIGKILL for the selected process.
.TP
.BR F1 , " Esc" , " q"
Show help, close the current view, or exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments.
.TP
.B 3
Resource not found.
.TP
.B 4
Permission denied.
.TP
.B 5
I/O, protocol, parse, or operation failure.
.TP
.B 6
Unsupported or unavailable capability.
.TP
.B 7
Conflict or ambiguous resource.
.TP
.B 8
Timeout.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted by Ctrl-C.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc
Process and system metrics source.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable ANSI colors when non-empty.
.SH SECURITY
LX never stores privilege passwords. Destructive operations are explicit,
validate protected targets, and conservatively confirm by default. Process
environment variables are not read; common secret command arguments are
redacted unless
.B --raw-command
is explicitly requested.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemd (1),
.BR proc (5)
